                                    [event_id] => 169
                                    [description] => <p><br />Welcome to Delhi International Airport! Upon arrival, our team  will assist you and transfer you to your pre-booked hotel by a private  chauffeur-driven vehicle.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><em><strong>New Delhi </strong>is the capital of India, has been  inhabited continuously since the 6th century BCE and has served as the  capital of multiple kingdoms and empires throughout its history. In the  1920s, it was redesigned by Sir Edwin Lutyens and Sir Herbert Baker.  Today, Delhi is a bustling metropolis that serves as a vibrant hub of  international arts and culture and a thriving commercial centre.</em></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Please note that the standard check-in time at hotels is 2 pm. As  most  international flights arrive in the evening or night, we do not  have any  sightseeing activities planned for the first day of the trip.  However,  if your flight arrives early in the day and you don\'t require  time to  recuperate from international travel, we can arrange some  sightseeing on  the first day of the trip at an additional charge.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MORNING: TOUR OF OLD &amp; NEW DELHI </strong></li>
</ul>
<p><br />Old  Delhi is known for its hustle and bustle, energy and colour,  local shops  and bazaars, and twists and turns of narrow lanes between  soaring  buildings and leaning houses. A rickshaw ride through the busy  and  narrow streets of Chandni Chowk will give you a unique experience.  Take a  look at the marvellous Jama Masjid, India&rsquo;s largest mosque.  Drive past  the Red Fort while enjoying the rickshaw ride. Your next  stop is Raj  Ghat, Mahatma Gandhi Memorial &ndash; a calm and quiet place in  complete  contrast to the commotion of the old walled city.<br /><br />Amongst  other  prominent attractions to cover on the same day is the  golden-domed  Gurudwara Bangla Sahib, a famous Sikh temple, which has a  huge kitchen  where the langar (free food for anyone who wants it) is  prepared for  thousands of people every day. Volunteers mostly staff it,  and you can  go in and look around or even help out.<br /><br />Visit the  inspiring  Mughal monument, Humayun&rsquo;s Tomb - UNESCO World Heritage Site.  It is the  first garden-tomb in the country, constructed in 1570.  Later, zip around  the Connaught Place shopping centre and be mesmerised  by the fast-paced  and upscale lifestyle. Take a look at the India Gate  and other princely  buildings, including Rashtrapati Bhavan, to admire  the architectural  grandeur of modern Delhi.<br /><br /><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">INCLUDED</span>:  Monument entrance fees, Rickshaw ride, personal  English-speaking  guide, private Chauffeur-driven vehicle &amp; Shoe  keeping charges at  the mosque.<span style=\"text-decoration: line-through;\"><br /></span></p>
                                )

